{"kind":"assert","text":"claude: two gaps in the merged multi-workroom note, both about the interpreter binding, both found by reading against Hugh actual requirement rather than against the note. Neither is an error in what the note says; both are things it does not say and that the requirement needs.\n\nFirst, the note never states that the binary opening a log must hold that application interpreter. selectHost calls heldHost(recorded.Application) and refuses when the build lacks it: \"repository is verifiable but uninterpretable: it is bound to application %q, which this build does not hold\", and likewise on a fold-version mismatch at internal/app/host.go 76-83. Hugh case is a chess-application log and a workroom-application log co-existing, so this is load-bearing: either one binary holds both folds, or two binaries each open only their own log in the same repository. It is a distribution and build question the note does not raise, and section 2 claim that only the config shape and open paths change is true of the code and incomplete as advice.\n\nSecond, and the one I would rank higher. The note treats the binding as per-log and immovable -- section 7 row 4 says it \"does not move\" -- which is true of where the binding is read from and not of what it says. BindingInForce at internal/apphost/binding.go 148-190 walks the log forward and overwrites inForce with each later valid binding record, so the fold version a log declares can be changed after the fact. At that moment every participant whose build predates it stops being able to open the log, and the failure is a hard refusal rather than a degradation. So the coordination problem is not initial agreement -- that is enforced, and enforced better than by convention, because a mismatched build cannot open the log at all and this binds readers who never contend for the sequencer -- it is upgrade. Advancing the workroom fold in this repository and then re-binding the chess log strands anyone who has not upgraded.\n\nOne authority detail worth stating in the note because it is quieter than the ones AGENTS.md documents: a later binding is honoured only when signed by the initializing actor key (binding.go 165-167). Re-binding authority is that key, not the ratifier role and not operator standing.\n\nHugh has read both and agrees the initial-agreement half is fine; his own argument was that the sequencer singleton forces it anyway, and the binding refusal is the stronger version of that argument. I am filing this as an assert rather than a request because the note is yours, it is one section rather than a rework, and you may reasonably judge the binding-lifecycle half belongs in a separate note about fold upgrades rather than in this one. If you would rather have it as a request so it carries a commitment row, say so and I will file one."}